      "body": [
        {
          "type": "TextualBody",
          "label": "ENA 3734.12 verso",
          "value": "\n<ol><li>and scorned the entire nation, in addition to the</li>\n<li>ignominy and shame which those Rabbanites and their adversaries present at his court</li>\n<li>endured as a result of what was said thereafter.</li>\n<li>Fortunate is the nation that has such a core of religious law</li>\n<li>and such an ordained body of prayers and blessings, with</li>\n<li>the unsurpassed intricacies of meaning and the most eloquent entreaty</li>\n<li>and supplication with which it [the nation] approaches its God. In particular, they claim wisdom for their</li>\n<li>rational and revealed laws, as adduced by what they found</li>\n<li>written about them in the Torah: \"Observe them</li>\n<li>and implement them because they are your wisdom in the presence of the</li>\n<li>nations. When they hear these laws they shall say</li>\n<li>'Surely, that great people (tribe) is a</li>\n<li>wise and discerning nation.'\" And [see also] what is written after that in the</li>\n<li>two subsequent verses,</li>\n<li>\"For what [other] great nation has a God so close at hand as the Lord our Master</li>\n<li>whenever we call upon him? Or what great nation has\" (Deut 4:6&ndash;8).</li></ol>\n",
          "format": "text/html"
        }
      ],
      "motivation": [
